Coastal wetlands, such as salt marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as natural buffers against the destructive forces of storms. Their ability to reduce flood damage is not merely about blocking water; it is a complex interaction of physics and ecology that dissipates energy before it reaches human infrastructure.

